+# Blocked: unique, required `users.email` cannot be applied to production
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` is written and works on a fresh database, but
+it **cannot be applied to the production snapshot** without changing customer
+rows, which Support owns and this change is explicitly forbidden from doing.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The snapshot (`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`) violates both new constraints:
+
+| Problem | Rows | Constraint broken |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `email` is `NULL` | id 17 (`Goran Okafor`) | `NOT NULL` |
+| Exact duplicate `ana.silva@example.com` | ids 1 and 23 | `UNIQUE` |
+| Case-only duplicate `ana.silva@example.com` vs `Ana.Silva@example.com` | ids 1/23 and 4 | `UNIQUE ... COLLATE NOCASE` |
+
+Adding `NOT NULL` and case-insensitive uniqueness requires the existing data to
+already satisfy them. There is no SQLite DDL that adds these constraints to a
+populated table without either (a) rebuilding and re-inserting every row, which
+fails on the rows above, or (b) updating/deleting the offending rows first.
+Option (b) is out of scope by instruction: the migration must not modify or
+delete any customer rows.
+
+## What was not done (deliberately)
+
+No workaround was used to force the snapshot test to pass, e.g. a partial unique
+index whose `WHERE` clause excludes the existing duplicates, or triggers that
+ignore pre-existing rows. That would leave production without the guarantee the
+migration is supposed to provide while making the test suite green.
+
+## State of the code
+
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes: after migration, inserting a NULL email
+  or a case-variant duplicate raises `IntegrityError`.
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` fails at `db.apply(conn, "002_unique_email.sql")`
+  with an `IntegrityError` (duplicate email) because the copy of existing rows
+  cannot satisfy the new constraints. The failure happens before the table is
+  replaced, so the snapshot rows are left unchanged. This failure cannot be
+  fixed from inside the migration.
+
+## Unblocking
+
+Support must clean the data first, then this migration can be re-run:
+
+1. Give id 17 a real email (or decide how missing emails are handled).
+2. Merge or reissue the duplicated accounts (ids 1, 23, and the case variant
+   at id 4) so each address maps to one customer.
+
+Once the snapshot satisfies "one non-NULL email per customer, compared
+case-insensitively", `002_unique_email.sql` applies without altering any row.
diff --git a/migrations/002_unique_email.sql b/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
index 91e11a9..ed272ea 100644
--- a/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
+++ b/migrations/002_unique_email.sql
@@ -1,2 +1,23 @@
 -- 002: users.email must be present and unique, ignoring case.
--- TODO
+--
+-- SQLite cannot add NOT NULL or a UNIQUE constraint to an existing column, so
+-- rebuild the table. Rows are copied verbatim; this migration never rewrites
+-- or deletes customer data. If the existing data cannot satisfy the new
+-- constraints, the copy fails and the table is left untouched (see BLOCKED.md).
+
+BEGIN;
+
+CREATE TABLE users_rebuilt (
+    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
+    email TEXT NOT NULL COLLATE NOCASE UNIQUE,
+    name TEXT
+);
+
+INSERT INTO users_rebuilt (id, email, name)
+SELECT id, email, name FROM users;
+
+DROP TABLE users;
+
+ALTER TABLE users_rebuilt RENAME TO users;
+
+COMMIT;
